<p>Interpretation:</p>
<p>This boxplot shows the distribution of ratings for grape varieties with more than 1,200 reviews, ordered by their average rating and colored by average price(binned in $10 increments). Varieties such as Nebbiolo and Champagne Blend appear above the overall average rating (dashed line), indicating stronger ratings for expensive varieties. In contrast, varieties like Rose, Merlot, and White Blend tend to fall below the overall mean, though their distributions still overlap heavily with higher-rated types.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion and Wrap-Up</strong></p>
<p>Overall, price, country of origin, and grape variety all show some relationship with wine ratings, but none appear to strongly determine quality on their own. Higher-priced wines and certain varieties tend to receive slightly higher average ratings, though the differences are relatively small. One limitation of this analysis is the removal of the 100 most expensive wines, which may exclude meaningful high-end observations while reducing skew.</p>
<p><strong>Connection to Class Ideas</strong></p>
<p>These visualizations apply key ideas from class about effective data communication. Ordering categories by average rating improves clarity and makes comparisons easier, while boxplots display the variation rather than relying only on means. The reference lines provide context for interpreting above and below average ratings. Additionally, color encoding allows an extra variable (average price) to be shown without making the visualizing.</p>



